    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear offensive ceiling centered on the matchup against Sam Aldegheri. **Tyler Soderstrom** headlines the lineup with a robust 70.21 score, buoyed by the favorable SP_xERA of 5.54 and beneficial wind conditions. He is joined by **Max Muncy** (66.79) and **Henry Bolte** (65.40) in exploiting this vulnerability; the combination of the wind blowing out and the high-yield matchup makes this trio the backbone of our daily production. **James Wood** (64.47) remains an elite start despite the 30% rain risk, as his aggressive batting order position (#1) and significant park factor cushion ensure he stays in the lineup despite facing a competent arm in Bradish.\n\nThe optimizer\u2019s toughest choice today involved **Alex Freeland**, who posted a 48.60 but ultimately hit the bench due to his bottom-of-the-order projection (#9) and the depth of our middle infield options. While **Brice Turang** (59.86) and **Travis Bazzana** (50.14) lack the same raw power, their projected spot in the #2 hole provides a floor for R-category production that **Alex Freeland** simply cannot replicate from the ninth spot. Elsewhere, we are leaning heavily on platoon advantages; **Kyle Karros** (55.12) and **Austin Riley** (59.32) provide necessary stability against left-handed starters, though we must monitor the status of **Gabriel Moreno**, **Dalton Rushing**, **Brice Turang**, **Max Muncy**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Corbin Carroll**, **Henry Bolte**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Austin Riley**, as their official lineup placements remain pending.\n\nRisk management remains paramount, particularly regarding **Seiya Suzuki** (48.98) and **Corbin Carroll** (57.59), who are fighting through tough matchups against Rasmussen. While the base projections for both are solid, the elite skill of the opposing arm and the -6% park penalty dampen their outlook. We have opted to bench **Zach McKinstry** (39.84) and **Jose Caballero** (47.56) as they fell below our efficiency threshold or were squeezed out by the optimizer\u2019s preference for better-sequenced hitters. The roster is set to maximize daily efficiency, but if the weather in the **James Wood** game shifts or late lineup scratches occur, we will be forced to pivot to our secondary utility depth.",
